.TH MU2EDAQ\-DISKWATCHER.CONF 5 "September 2026" "mu2edaq-diskwatcher 1.3.0" "Mu2e DAQ Utilities"
.SH NAME
mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.conf \- configuration file for mu2edaq\-diskwatcher
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ./diskwatcher.yaml
.br
.B ./config/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.yaml
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher (1)
reads a YAML configuration file naming the paths to watch and how to alarm on
them.
The file has four top-level keys:
.BR watcher ", " files ", " paths " and " peers .
All four are optional; a file with none of them is valid and simply watches
nothing.
.PP
The file is loaded once at startup.
Problems found in it are reported as warnings on standard error, shown on the
.B /config
page and in
.BR /api/config ,
and do not prevent startup: a control-room daemon must keep running on a
partly bad configuration rather than leaving the experiment unmonitored.
The only fatal case is a file named explicitly with
.B \-\-config
that does not exist at all.
.PP
Unrecognised keys are reported rather than silently ignored, so that a typo
such as
.B warnign
does not quietly disable an alarm.
.SH THE watcher SECTION
Server settings.
Every key may be overridden by an environment variable and then by a
command-line option; see
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher (1).
.TP
.B web_host
Bind address for the web server.
Default:
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.B web_port
TCP port for the web server.
Default:
.BR 5002 .
.TP
.B poll_interval
Seconds between polls of every watched path.
Default:
.BR 30 .
.TP
.B default_delay
Staleness threshold applied to entries that specify neither
.B delay
nor a threshold block.
Default:
.BR 300 .
.TP
.B daemon
Run in the background.
Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.B run_dir
Per-node directory for everything the process writes: the pid file, the log,
and any history or database a future version keeps.
May contain placeholders; see
.BR "PATH PLACEHOLDERS" .
Default:
.BR run/{host} ,
relative to the working directory.
The checkout is commonly on NFS and shared by several DAQ nodes, so the
default keys the directory by short hostname and two nodes never write the
same file.
.TP
.B pid_file
Where the daemon writes its process ID.
May contain placeholders.
Default:
.BR {run_dir}/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.pid ,
applied in daemon mode only; a foreground run writes no pid file.
.B start\-mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.sh
passes
.B \-\-run\-dir
and
.B \-\-pid\-file
for the same location, so the script and the daemon always agree on where to
look; see
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher (1).
.TP
.B log_file
Where the daemon's standard output and error are appended.
May contain placeholders.
Default:
.BR {run_dir}/mu2edaq\-diskwatcher.log ,
applied in daemon mode only.
.TP
.B peer_timeout
Seconds allowed for one peer's HTTP round trip, unless the peer entry sets its
own
.BR timeout .
Default:
.BR 5 .
.TP
.B peer_interval
Seconds between fetches of every peer.
Default: the value of
.BR poll_interval .
.PP
.nf
watcher:
  web_host:      "0.0.0.0"
  web_port:      5002
  poll_interval: 30
  default_delay: 300
  daemon:        false
  run_dir:       "run/{host}"
  pid_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.pid"
  log_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.log"
  peer_timeout:  5
.fi
.SH PATH PLACEHOLDERS
.BR run_dir ", " pid_file " and " log_file
may contain the following, expanded once at startup after every configuration
layer has been applied:
.TP
.B {host}
The short hostname, e.g.
.BR mu2e\-dl\-01 .
.TP
.B {hostname}
The hostname exactly as the system reports it, possibly fully qualified.
.TP
.B {port}
The web server port.
.TP
.B {user}
The account running the process.
.TP
.B {run_dir}
The resolved
.BR run_dir .
Valid in
.BR pid_file " and " log_file
only.
.PP
An unrecognised placeholder is left in the path unchanged, so it stays
visible on the
.B /config
page rather than silently disappearing.
The parent directory of the pid file and the log file is created if it does
not exist.
.SH WATCH ENTRIES
.B files
and
.B paths
are lists of entries.
Entries under
.B files
are treated as regular files; entries under
.B paths
are treated as directories.
.PP
An entry is normally a mapping.
A bare string is also accepted and is equivalent to a mapping with only
.B path
set.
.TP
.B path
The absolute or relative path to watch.
Required; an entry without it is dropped with a warning.
.TP
.B delay
Staleness threshold in seconds.
Optional; see
.B THE delay KEY
below.
.TP
.B label
Name shown on the dashboard instead of the raw path.
Defaults to the path.
.TP
.B ssh
Watch the path on a remote host.
See
.BR "THE ssh BLOCK" .
.TP
.B space
Free-space thresholds.
Valid on
.B paths
entries only; on a
.B files
entry it is reported and ignored.
See
.BR "THE space BLOCK" .
.TP
.B size
File-size thresholds.
Valid on
.B files
entries only; on a
.B paths
entry it is reported and ignored.
See
.BR "THE size BLOCK" .
.PP
A directory's modification time changes when files are added to or removed from
it, but not when a file inside it is written.
To detect writes, list the file itself under
.BR files .
.SH THE delay KEY
.B delay
is optional, and its absence means different things depending on the rest of
the entry:
.PP
.nf
    delay: N          monitor staleness with a threshold of N seconds
    delay: null       do not monitor staleness
    absent, and the entry has a space: or size: block
                      do not monitor staleness
    absent, and the entry has neither
                      use watcher.default_delay
.fi
.PP
The last case preserves the behaviour of versions before 1.2.0, so an existing
configuration keeps alarming exactly as it did.
.PP
An entry that monitors nothing at all \(em no
.BR delay ,
no
.BR space ,
no
.B size
\(em is reported as a warning and merely listed on the dashboard.
Entries with no staleness threshold are shown as
.I unmonitored
on the Watcher page and are excluded from the OK and STALE counts.
.SH SIZE UNITS
Thresholds accept a number with an optional unit suffix.
Suffixes are case-insensitive; a bare number means bytes.
.PP
.TS
tab(|) box;
lb | lb | lb
l  | l  | l .
Suffix|Multiplier|Family
_
(none), B|1|bytes
KB, K|1000|SI
MB, M|1000^2|SI
GB, G|1000^3|SI
TB, T|1000^4|SI
PB, P|1000^5|SI
KiB|1024|IEC
MiB|1024^2|IEC
GiB|1024^3|IEC
TiB|1024^4|IEC
PiB|1024^5|IEC
N%|percent|space: only
.TE
.PP
So
.B 500 GiB
is 536870912000 bytes while
.B 500 GB
is 500000000000 bytes.
The dashboards always
.I display
IEC units, so a threshold configured as
.B 2TB
is shown as
.BR "1.8 TiB" .
.PP
In a
.B space
block a percentage means
.I percent of total capacity still free:
.B "warning: 10%"
fires once free space drops to ten percent of the filesystem or below.
.PP
A file has no total capacity, so a percentage in a
.B size
block is rejected.
That threshold is dropped with a warning; the other thresholds in the block
continue to apply.
.SH THE space BLOCK
Enables free-space monitoring for the filesystem holding a watched directory.
The entry then appears on the
.B /space
dashboard and in
.BR /api/space .
.TP
.B warning
Free space at or below this value is WARNING.
.TP
.B critical
Free space at or below this value is CRITICAL.
.TP
.B full
Free space at or below this value is FULL.
.PP
All three are optional and any subset works.
An empty block, or one whose thresholds all fail to parse, disables free-space
monitoring for that entry with a warning.
.PP
Because the thresholds state how much space must remain
.IR free ,
they descend:
.BR "warning > critical > full" .
Each is inclusive on the bad side, so free space exactly equal to
.B warning
is already WARNING.
States are evaluated most-severe-first, so a partially configured or even a
misordered block still yields a defined answer.
.PP
Misordered thresholds are reported and marked on the dashboard but never
silently corrected; the effect is that some alarm states become unreachable.
When a block mixes percentages with absolute sizes the ordering depends on the
filesystem's capacity and is therefore checked at poll time, and reported
through the
.B space_limits_ordered
field.
.PP
.nf
paths:
  - path:  /data/mu2e
    delay: 300
    label: "Main data"
    space:
      warning:  "10%"        # percent of capacity still free
      critical: "500 GiB"    # percentages and sizes may be mixed
      full:     "50 GiB"
.fi
.SH THE size BLOCK
Enables size monitoring for a watched file.
The entry then appears on the
.B /sizes
dashboard and in
.BR /api/sizes .
.TP
.B warning
Size at or above this value is WARNING.
.TP
.B critical
Size at or above this value is CRITICAL.
.TP
.BR full ", " max
Size at or above this value is FULL.
.B max
is an accepted synonym.
.TP
.B allow_empty
When
.BR true ,
a zero-length file is not an alarm.
Default:
.BR false .
.PP
Because the thresholds state how large the file may grow, they ascend:
.BR "warning < critical < full" .
.PP
A zero-length file is EMPTY, checked
.I before
any threshold, so a truncated file alarms whatever its limits say.
A block containing only
.B allow_empty: false
and no thresholds is valid and useful: it means "alarm if this file is ever
zero bytes".
.PP
.nf
files:
  - path:  /data/mu2e/run_latest.dat
    delay: 120
    label: "Latest run data"
    size:
      warning:  "1 GiB"
      critical: "4 GiB"
      full:     "8 GiB"
      allow_empty: false

  # alarm only on a zero-length file
  - path:  /data/mu2e/index.db
    label: "Run index"
    size:
      allow_empty: false

  # legitimately empty most of the time, but should not grow without bound
  - path:  /var/spool/mu2e/errors.log
    label: "Error spool"
    size:
      allow_empty: true
      warning:  "1 MiB"
      critical: "10 MiB"
.fi
.SH THE ssh BLOCK
Watches the path on a remote host.
Only
.B host
is required.
.TP
.B host
Remote hostname, optionally
.IR user @ host .
.TP
.B port
SSH port.
Default:
.BR 22 .
.TP
.B key
Private key file.
A leading
.B ~
is expanded.
Default: the SSH agent and the usual identity files.
.TP
.B timeout
Connection and command timeout in seconds.
Default:
.BR 10 .
.TP
.B options
Extra
.B ssh
options, as a list or a single string.
.PP
Authentication is non-interactive
.RB ( BatchMode=yes ),
so a key that requires a passphrase must already be loaded into an agent.
.PP
One SSH round trip per entry retrieves the modification time, the size and,
for directories, the filesystem usage.
The probe runs a short
.B python3
snippet, which behaves identically on Linux, macOS and BSD remotes.
A host without
.B python3
falls back to
.BR stat (1),
trying GNU
.RB ( "\-c '%Y %s'" )
and then BSD
.RB ( "\-f '%m %z'" )
syntax; the fallback yields the modification time and size but not disk usage,
so free-space monitoring on such a host reports UNKNOWN.
The choice is cached per host for the life of the process.
.PP
.nf
    ssh:
      host:    daq-node01.example.com
      port:    22
      key:     ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
      timeout: 10
      options:
        - StrictHostKeyChecking=no
.fi
.SH THE peers SECTION
A list of other
.B mu2edaq\-diskwatcher
instances whose entries are shown on this instance's dashboards as well.
Each peer's
.B /api/state
is fetched over HTTP every
.B watcher.peer_interval
seconds, and its entries appear on the Watcher, Disk Space and File Sizes
pages under a heading carrying the peer's label, URL, hostname and version.
The summary cards count local entries plus those of every reachable peer.
.PP
An item is a mapping, or a bare URL string equivalent to a mapping with only
.B url
set.
.TP
.B url
Base URL of the peer, for example
.BR http://mu2e\-dl\-01.fnal.gov:5002 .
Required.
The scheme may be omitted, in which case
.B http
is assumed; a trailing slash is ignored.
A value that is not an HTTP or HTTPS URL is dropped with a warning.
.TP
.B label
Name shown in the group heading.
Default: the host and port from the URL.
.TP
.B timeout
Seconds allowed for the HTTP round trip.
Default:
.BR watcher.peer_timeout .
.TP
.B enabled
When
.BR false ,
the peer is listed on
.B /config
but never fetched.
Default:
.BR true .
.PP
A peer's states are shown exactly as that peer evaluated them against its own
thresholds; they are not recomputed here.
Aggregation is one hop: a peer is asked for its own entries only, so two
instances may list each other without looping.
A peer whose URL resolves back to this very instance is recognised by its
.B instance_id
and refused.
.PP
While a peer is unreachable its entries from the last successful fetch remain
on screen, dimmed, together with the time they were last current, and are
excluded from the summary counts.
An unreachable peer does not change this instance's own
.B /api/health
status.
.PP
The list can be replaced from the environment
.RB ( MU2EDAQ_DISKWATCHER_PEERS )
or the command line
.RB ( \-\-peer ", " \-\-no\-peers );
see
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher (1).
.PP
.nf
peers:
  - url:     http://mu2e-dl-01.fnal.gov:5002
    label:   "mu2e-dl-01"
    timeout: 5
  - mu2e-dl-02.fnal.gov:5002       # bare form; label defaults to host:port
.fi
.SS Finding peers with discovery
Instead of, or as well as, listing peers,
.B peers
may be a mapping with two keys.
.B static
holds the list described above.
.B discover
makes this instance scan for other diskwatchers with
.BR mu2edaq\-discovery (1)
\(em every diskwatcher answers its multicast queries \(em and federate
whatever answers.
Writing the block turns discovery on unless it says
.BR "enabled: false" .
.TP
.B enabled
Default:
.B true
when the block is present.
.TP
.B filter
A mapping of fnmatch globs matched against the responder's
.BR app ", " name " and " host .
.B app
defaults to
.B diskwatcher
and should be left so; add
.B host
to restrict federation to a set of nodes.
.TP
.B interval
Seconds between scans.
Default:
.BR watcher.peer_interval .
.TP
.B timeout
Seconds one scan waits for replies.
Default:
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B grace
Seconds a peer may go unanswered before it is dropped.
Multicast replies do get lost, so a peer is kept, and still fetched, through
missed scans; only after
.B grace
seconds without an answer is it removed.
Default: three scan intervals.
.TP
.B exclude
Host globs never to federate, matched against both the full and the short
hostname.
.PP
Discovery decides which peers are
.IR listed ;
each peer's own HTTP fetch decides whether it is
.IR up .
A discovered peer that also appears under
.B static
takes that entry's label, timeout and
.BR enabled .
This instance's own responder is recognised by its
.B instance_id
and never federated.
Each peer's origin is reported as
.B source
.RB ( static " or " discovered )
in
.B /api/peers
and on the dashboards.
.PP
The package is optional: if
.B discover
is enabled and
.B mu2edaq\-discovery
is not installed, a warning appears on
.B /config
and static peers keep working.
Multicast must be routed between the nodes concerned.
.PP
.nf
peers:
  static:
    - url:   http://mu2e-dl-01.fnal.gov:5002
      label: "mu2e-dl-01"
  discover:
    filter:   {app: diskwatcher, host: "mu2e-dl-*"}
    interval: 60
    timeout:  2.0
    grace:    180
    exclude:  ["mu2e-dl-99"]
.fi
.SH ALARM STATES
.SS Staleness (the / page)
.BR ok ", " stale ", " missing ", " unmonitored .
.SS Free space (the /space page)
.BR GOOD ", " WARNING ", " CRITICAL ", " FULL ", " MISSING ", " UNKNOWN .
.SS File size (the /sizes page)
.BR GOOD ", " EMPTY ", " WARNING ", " CRITICAL ", " FULL ", " MISSING ", "
.BR UNKNOWN .
.PP
.B MISSING
means the path itself could not be read.
.B UNKNOWN
means the path exists but the measurement failed \(em for example a remote host
where disk usage could not be obtained.
The distinction matters when deciding whether to page someone.
.SH EXAMPLE
A complete file:
.PP
.nf
watcher:
  web_host:      "0.0.0.0"
  web_port:      5002
  poll_interval: 30
  default_delay: 300
  daemon:        false
  run_dir:       "run/{host}"          # per node: the checkout is on NFS
  pid_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.pid"
  log_file:      "{run_dir}/mu2edaq-diskwatcher.log"

files:
  - path:  /var/log/messages
    delay: 3600
    label: "System log"

  - path:  /data/mu2e/run_latest.dat
    delay: 120
    label: "Latest run data"
    size:
      warning:  "1 GiB"
      critical: "4 GiB"
      full:     "8 GiB"

  - path:  /data/mu2e/remote.dat
    delay: 120
    label: "Remote data"
    ssh:
      host: daq-node01.example.com

paths:
  - path:  /data/mu2e
    delay: 300
    label: "Main data"
    space:
      warning:  "10%"
      critical: "500 GiB"
      full:     "50 GiB"

  - path:  /scratch
    label: "Scratch volume"    # free space only, no staleness alarm
    space:
      warning:  "2 TiB"
      critical: "500 GiB"
      full:     "50 GiB"

peers:
  - url:   http://daq-node02.example.com:5002
    label: "daq-node02"
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mu2edaq\-diskwatcher (1),
.BR ssh_config (5),
.BR stat (1),
.BR df (1)
